WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.201
Drone operations combine aviation safety, mission planning, technical systems, regulation, data handling, and responsible judgment.

00:00:08.201 --> 00:00:16.401
The aircraft, controller, batteries, sensors, payloads, software, links, crew, and records form one operational system.

00:00:16.401 --> 00:00:29.075
Strong missions define purpose, review location, current official rules, airspace, weather, obstacles, people, property, battery margins, launch and recovery, and emergency options before takeoff.

00:00:29.075 --> 00:00:33.797
Conservative go or no-go decisions protect the mission.

00:00:33.797 --> 00:00:42.991
During flight, maintain awareness, visual control as required, clear crew communication, and sufficient battery and signal margins.

00:00:42.991 --> 00:00:49.204
Respond early to changing weather, low battery, unexpected behavior, or hazards.

00:00:49.204 --> 00:00:53.429
Recovery and post-flight inspection are planned phases.

00:00:53.429 --> 00:01:04.114
Payloads should match the mission, and data collection should be accurate, minimal, privacy-conscious, securely handled, and linked to flight records.

00:01:04.114 --> 00:01:09.333
Logs and after-action reviews support maintenance, accountability, and improvement.

00:01:09.333 --> 00:01:14.552
The goal is safe, lawful, useful, and accountable operation.

00:01:14.552 --> 00:01:28.716
Good operators follow current rules, respect people and property, protect data, understand automation limits, and stop rather than force a flight when conditions or authority are inadequate.
